 "summary": "## katG (Rv1908c) \u2014 *Mycobacterium tuberculosis* H37Rv\n\nBoth structured channels resolved the identifier cleanly: KEGG resolved `katG` by symbol to **mtu:Rv1908c**, and STRING resolved it to **83332.Rv1908c** (preferred name `katG`). No resolution failures or empty-record ambiguities in either result.\n\n### Function\n\nKEGG's pathway assignments are the informative evidence here. katG is placed in **mtu00360 (Phenylalanine metabolism)** and **mtu00380 (Tryptophan metabolism)** \u2014 the pathway memberships characteristic of a **catalase-peroxidase (heme-dependent peroxidase)**, which is how KEGG maps this enzyme class into aromatic amino acid catabolism. It is also placed in **mtu00983 (Drug metabolism \u2013 other enzymes)**, consistent with its second, clinically dominant role: enzymatic activation/conversion of a drug substrate rather than a housekeeping metabolic step.\n\nSo the annotation supported by the tools is: a peroxidatic enzyme with both an oxidative-stress-detoxification/peroxidase metabolic role and a drug-metabolizing role.\n\nThe remaining two KEGG hits, **mtu01100 (Metabolic pathways)** and **mtu01110 (Biosynthesis of secondary metabolites)**, are genome-scale container maps and say essentially nothing specific about this gene; I list them only for completeness.\n\n### Interaction partners\n\nI split these by evidence class, because most of the high-scoring STRING network around katG is literature co-mention.\n\n**Partners with support beyond textmining (the substantive ones):**\n\n- **83332.Rv1909c (furA)** \u2014 combined 0.979, with a strong **neighborhood score of 0.829**. furA is the immediately adjacent gene, and conserved genomic adjacency is genuine non-literature evidence. This is the single best-supported association in the network.\n- **83332.Rv1907c** \u2014 combined 0.957, neighborhood 0.805 **and coexpression 0.731**, with only weak textmining (0.254). This is the most \"honest\" partner in the set: it is essentially unstudied in the literature yet scores highly on two independent structural/expression channels. katG sits in a conserved, co-expressed locus with Rv1907c and furA.\n- **83332.Rv3846 (sodA)** and **83332.Rv0432 (sodC)** \u2014 combined 0.973 and 0.936, each carrying a **database score of 0.5** (curated pathway co-membership) alongside high textmining. Superoxide dismutases are functional companions of a catalase-peroxidase in reactive-oxygen detoxification; the database channel supports co-membership, not physical binding.\n- **83332.Rv3566c (nat)**, **83332.Rv1600 (hisC1)**, **83332.Rv3772 (hisC2)**, **83332.Rv2231c (cobC)** \u2014 all with **database score 0.9** and negligible textmining (0.09\u20130.15). These are curated pathway co-memberships, most plausibly reflecting shared KEGG metabolic maps rather than any direct interaction. Note that hisC1/hisC2/cobC are aminotransferases, which fits co-membership in the aromatic amino acid metabolism maps above.\n\n**Partners supported essentially by textmining alone** \u2014 the tool's own notes flag these: ahpC, pncA, embB, ndh, kasA, rpoB, gyrA, inhA, gyrB, fabG1, embA, rpsL, i.e. **83332.Rv2428, 83332.Rv2043c, 83332.Rv3795, 83332.Rv1854c, 83332.Rv2245, 83332.Rv0667, 83332.Rv0006, 83332.Rv1484, 83332.Rv3566c**\u2026 (correction: nat/Rv3566c belongs to the database-supported group above) \u2026**83332.Rv0005, 83332.Rv1483, 83332.Rv3794, 83332.Rv0682**.\n\nThese score 0.89\u20130.97 combined but have max non-textmining scores of 0.04\u20130.10. That pattern is diagnostic: this is the *M. tuberculosis* drug-resistance gene panel (rpoB, gyrA/gyrB, rpsL, embA/embB, pncA, inhA/fabG1/kasA, ndh), co-mentioned with katG in resistance-genotyping papers. These are **not** evidence of functional or physical interaction \u2014 they are evidence that katG is co-reported with other resistance determinants. I would not annotate any of them as a katG partner. ahpC (83332.Rv2428) is a plausible functional relative on peroxide-detoxification grounds, but STRING gives it *no* non-textmining support (max 0.096), so on this evidence it stands as co-mention only.\n\n### Summary\n\nkatG/Rv1908c is a catalase-peroxidase acting in peroxidase-linked aromatic amino acid metabolism (mtu00360, mtu00380) and in drug metabolism (mtu00983). Its only well-supported local associations are with its genomic neighbours **furA (83332.Rv1909c)** and **83332.Rv1907c**, plus curated co-membership with the superoxide dismutases **sodA (83332.Rv3846)** and **sodC (83332.Rv0432)**. Its large high-scoring resistance-gene neighbourhood in STRING is a literature artefact and should not be read as functional interaction. I did not query PubMed, as the structured channels left no question open that literature could settle without adding noise of exactly the kind already visible in the textmining channel.",
